Looking at IEEE 754 double-precision format:
- Sign bit: 1 bit
- Exponent: 11 bits (bits 52-62)
- Fraction: 52 bits (bits 0-51)

The condition "high 32 bits (excluding sign bit) >= 0x7ff00000" means:
- We're looking at bits 1-31 (31 bits) of the high 32-bit word
- In IEEE 754 double, the high 32 bits contain: sign bit + 11 exponent bits + first 20 fraction bits
- 0x7ff00000 in binary is: 0111 1111 1111 0000 0000 0000 0000 0000
- So we need the exponent bits (11 bits) to be all 1s (0x7ff), and the first few fraction bits to be >= 0x000

This corresponds to special values in IEEE 754:
- Exponent = 0x7ff (all 1s) means either infinity or NaN
- If fraction = 0: infinity (positive or negative depending on sign bit)
- If fraction != 0: NaN

The key challenge is finding a string representation that atof() will parse to produce such a value.
